# The File System (fs)

The `fs` module allows you to work with the file system on your computer.

### Reading Files
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

// Asynchronous
fs.readFile('input.txt', (err, data) => {
  if (err) return console.error(err);
  console.log(data.toString());
});
```

### Writing Files
```javascript
fs.writeFile('output.txt', 'Hello Node!', (err) => {
  if (err) console.log(err);
});
```

# Callbacks & Promises

### Callbacks
The traditional way of handling asynchronous operations in Node.js. Can lead to "callback hell".

### Promises
A cleaner alternative to callbacks. A Promise represents the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation.

```javascript
const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // async logic
});
```

# Async/Await

Introduced in ES2017, `async/await` is syntactic sugar built on top of Promises, making asynchronous code look and behave more like synchronous code.

```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const response = await someAsyncCall();
    console.log(response);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
}
```

# Event Emitters

Much of the Node.js core API is built around an idiomatic asynchronous event-driven architecture in which certain objects (called "emitters") emit named events that cause Function objects ("listeners") to be called.

```javascript
const EventEmitter = require('events');
const myEmitter = new EventEmitter();

myEmitter.on('event', () => {
  console.log('an event occurred!');
});

myEmitter.emit('event');
```

# Introduction to Express

Express is a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework that provides a robust set of features for web and mobile applications.

### Basic Server
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(3000);
```

# Middleware in Express

Middleware functions are functions that have access to the request object (`req`), the response object (`res`), and the next middleware function in the application’s request-response cycle.

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log('Time:', Date.now());
  next();
});
```

# Routing & Parameters

Routing refers to determining how an application responds to a client request to a particular endpoint.

### Route Parameters
```javascript
app.get('/users/:userId', (req, res) => {
  res.send(req.params.userId);
});
```

### Query Parameters
Access via `req.query` (e.g., `/search?q=node`).
